OpenSSL 4.1.0: SIGILL while loading on AArch64 Linux that reports SVE2 without SVE (Android emulators on Apple M4)

Upstream code: the crypto/armcap.c change of OpenSSL commit openssl/openssl@cb5f5d00e4 (pull request #33481), verbatim, as
merged to master on 2026-11-04 and to openssl-4.1 as openssl/openssl@658a1c947b; only the hunk's line numbers differ, as
the openssl-4.1 branch has the Neoverse N3/V3 lines above it that 4.1.0 lacks. The tenth patch carried under ADR-0019
(docs/adr/0019-openssl-4-and-ech.md), applied after openssl-4.1.0-ech-alpn-padding.patch; pinned by its SHA-256 in
gradle/openssl.toml as patch_10, applied by tools/openssl/apply-patch.pl to the verified tarball, and dropped with OpenSSL
4.1.1, the first release that has the commit.

The bug: OPENSSL_cpuid_setup(), a constructor of libcrypto (and so of libksl, which links it), calls
_armv8_sve_get_vl_bytes(), which executes the SVE instruction cntb, whenever the kernel reports HWCAP2_SVE2. Linux
kernels without commit 064737920bdb ("arm64: Filter out SVE hwcaps when FEAT_SVE isn't implemented") report HWCAP2_SVE2
without HWCAP_SVE on a CPU with SME but no SVE, such as a guest on Apple M4: the Android 14 emulator's /proc/cpuinfo
there lists sve2, svei8mm and svebf16 and the sme features, but not sve. The library then dies with SIGILL in its
constructor while System.loadLibrary loads it, and the app's process with it. The fix probes the vector length only
when both ARMV8_SVE and ARMV9_SVE2 are set; ARMV9_SVE2_POLY1305 is set only inside that block, and the ChaCha20 SVE
path already checks ARMV8_SVE first, so no SVE code runs without SVE.

--- a/crypto/armcap.c
+++ b/crypto/armcap.c
@@ -461,7 +461,12 @@
             || MIDR_IS_CPU_MODEL(OPENSSL_arm_midr, ARM_CPU_IMP_NVIDIA, NVIDIA_CPU_PART_OLYMPUS)))
         OPENSSL_armcap_P |= ARMV8_HAVE_SHA3_AND_WORTH_USING;
 
-    if (OPENSSL_armcap_P & ARMV9_SVE2) {
+    /*
+     * _armv8_sve_get_vl_bytes() executes an SVE instruction. Some kernels and
+     * hypervisors report SVE2 without SVE (e.g. guests on Apple M4, which has
+     * SME but no SVE), so require both before probing the vector length.
+     */
+    if ((OPENSSL_armcap_P & ARMV8_SVE) && (OPENSSL_armcap_P & ARMV9_SVE2)) {
         uint64_t vl_bytes = _armv8_sve_get_vl_bytes();
 
         if (vl_bytes > 16 && (vl_bytes & (vl_bytes - 1)) == 0) {
